  "summary": "Fast muzzle flashes, sprite demons and M16s — the shooter look the revival keeps choosing.",
  "description": "The Doom clone era (retroactively \"boomer shooter\") defined shooter visuals: 2.5D levels of shifting-sector architecture, sprite enemies billboarded in fake 3D, brown-and-grey textures lit by muzzle flash, HUDs with grinning status faces, and heavy-metal MIDIs. Doom (1993), Build-engine games (Duke Nukem 3D, Blood) and Quake's true-3D upgrade composed a grammar of speed, gore, and readable level color-coding (red=keys). The 2019+ revival (Dusk, Ion Fury, Wrath, Selaco) rebuilt the look with modern engines, and its fans kept the HUDs, the wall textures and the joy of the strafe.",
  "culturalContext": "The Doom clone era shaped gaming's most durable grammar — speed, strafing, readable level color-coding — and its 2019+ \"boomer shooter\" revival (Dusk, Ion Fury) turned the aesthetic into heritage: modern indie studios deliberately resurrect the HUDs and sprite logic as genre authenticity, and the era's soundtracks anchor its nostalgia economy.",
